+## Confirmed findings
+- [Blocking] §2.3 Status table, `unknown` row; Key abstractions LoadedInstructions "a record older than `since` is `Unknown`" — The `unknown` rule misclassifies nearly every fresh session: the hook writes the record before session-report/observer stamps `occupant.since`, so new sessions read `unknown` instead of `current` on the production path.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: src/bootstrap/setup_claude.rs:172 runs `seat --hook-prompt; session-report --from-hook claude` in sequence, so the record's `at` is stamped first. src/transcripts/capture.rs:85-105 `report_args` carries no timestamp; src/observe/mutations.rs:655-694 sets `at = a.at.unwrap_or(cx.now)` and `Occupant{since: at}`. So record.at < occupant.since on every new session or /clear. Codex has no session report; its occupant comes from the observer diff, a race the spec never orders. Spec offers no tolerance window, no session-id binding (record schema has no ns field), no ordering guarantee; "Honest limits" does not cover this path.
+  severity note: floor applies — the feature's stated core purpose (show whether loaded instructions are stale) is defeated on the normal start path; the low-blast-radius profile does not demote it.
+  fix-shape hint: bind the record to the session instead of a timestamp (store the native session id, or the pane, in the record and compare with the occupant), or compare `at` against something committed before the hook runs, or define a grace window; state the production write order explicitly.
+- [Should-fix] §2.3 "Who writes it" (`seat --instructions`; daemon `seat.resolve` for `seat --json`); LoadedInstructions Lifecycle "last writer wins" — Making `seat --json`/`seat --instructions` writers of the single last-writer-wins record lets a metadata read or operator invocation overwrite the hook record, flipping a stale or truncated session to `current`/untruncated although the agent's context never received the text.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: templates/software-factory/AGENTS.md:38 tells agents to run `herdr-graph seat --json` for bead labels; src/bootstrap/tests.rs:1040 asserts the seat skill contains that command. A `seat --json | jq .beads_labels` after a HEAD edit rewrites rev, digests, `omitted: []` and `source: seat-json`, so roster reports `current` and untruncated while the model holds the old capped hook text. Spec has no precedence rule and no per-source history. Refute seat's point ("injected, not retained") was conceded by the ground seat as a mitigating factor; it does not cover a `seat --json` read that hands no text to the model.
+  fix-shape hint: let only hook sources set or clear `truncated` and advance `rev`, or keep one record per source, or make `seat --json` non-writing.
+- [Should-fix] §2.3 record `parts`/`omitted`/`cut`; Status `current` "every part at HEAD has the same label and digest as the record" — The spec does not define how omitted/cut parts participate in the staleness comparison, so truncated seats read permanently stale or report changes to parts the agent never received.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: §2.3 record example lists "global rules/style.md" in both `parts` and `omitted`; the Status table never says whether omitted parts are excluded; the `truncated` row says only "reported separately"; the digest is the SHA-256 of the full rendered body, so a cut part never matches either way. Reading 1 (`parts` = kept only) makes truncated seats permanently `stale (added)`; reading 2 (`parts` = full manifest) reports `stale` for a part the agent never held and that a reload would truncate again. Refute seat concedes the omitted-part edit reads `stale` and calls it a one-sentence doc fix.
+  fix-shape hint: one rule in §2.3: `parts` is the full manifest, omitted parts are excluded from the comparison, and the cut part compares on its delivered prefix (or is reported via `truncated` only); add a test to hg-blw.4.
+- [Nit] Key abstractions Scope "resolves retired targets too … reuses `instruction_source`'s `pick` logic and error wording"; hg-blw.7 acceptance — Scope must resolve retired targets but reuses `pick`, which returns a Retired error when only retired objects match; the spec gives no rule for several retired matches with no live one.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: src/store/instruction_source.rs:259-285 `pick`: `([], Some(_)) => Err(InstructionError::Retired{..})`. Spec line 440 says reuse `pick`; line 484 says `instructions show` behaviour is unchanged; line 436 says Scope resolves retired targets. Scope's error list (line 441) omits Retired, which shows the intent but not the mechanism.
+  severity note: both refute and ground seats read the intent as unambiguous (retired-only resolves; several retired is Ambiguous), so Nit.
+  fix-shape hint: one sentence: `pick` gains a retired-allowed mode used by Scope; unique retired resolves, several retired-only matches are Ambiguous.
+- [Nit] §2.3 Digest choice ("their `path` is `seat.toml` or `template.toml`"); Status `stale` row "one `git log -1` per changed path" — Attributing the 'changed' commit by path makes seat-section and responsibility parts cite whatever unrelated commit last rewrote the record (rename, lifecycle, runtime).
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: §3 Rejected table (line 390) rejects blob ids because unrelated record edits would show as drift; the `changed` column then uses last-touch by path on the same record files. src/observe/mutations.rs:745-747 rewrites seat.toml for reload_required and lifecycle. Staleness itself stays correct (digest-driven); only the cited commit is loose.
+  fix-shape hint: label the column "record last touched", or walk back to the first commit where the part's rendered digest differs from the recorded one.
+- [Nit] Key abstractions Inventory "Errors: `StoreError` only … maps to exit 6"; §2.2 Cost "one render per active seat" — The views have no per-object error isolation: one seat whose stack fails to resolve maps to a whole-command exit 6, blanking the roster when it is needed; behaviour for malformed history records is unspecified.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: src/bootstrap/instructions.rs:105-131 `template_participation` returns `StoreError` via `unresolved(...)` for a dangling template_ref/application; roster calls the manifest for every active seat (spec line 212, 493); the `instructions` JSON object (lines 186, 450) has no error state. Line 459 gives the loaded-record file an `Unknown` fallback but nothing equivalent exists for the render. HistoryWalker (line 467) does not cover a commit with a malformed OperationRecord.
+  demoted: profile states a single-operator local tool where `show`, `list` and `seat --instructions` already expose the exact error for a broken seat, so a whole-roster exit 6 is a Nit here.
+  fix-shape hint: a per-seat `instructions: {status: "error", message}` row, plus a skip-with-warning rule for malformed history records.
+- [Nit] §2.3 harness files "in the seat's cwd … and its ancestors: … `AGENTS.md`, `AGENTS.override.md`"; user-level `$CODEX_HOME/AGENTS.md` — The Codex file discovery is misrepresented: Codex walks from the project root down to cwd, takes one file per directory with the override shadowing AGENTS.md, reads `$CODEX_HOME/AGENTS.override.md`, and caps at 32 KiB; none of this is modelled.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: https://learn.chatgpt.com/docs/agent-configuration/agents-md (redirect from developers.openai.com/codex/guides/agents-md): "Codex reads `AGENTS.override.md` if it exists. Otherwise, Codex reads `AGENTS.md`" (global); "Starting at the project root (typically the Git root), Codex walks down to your current working directory"; "Codex includes at most one file per directory"; `project_doc_max_bytes` default 32 KiB. Spec's unbounded ancestor walk lists AGENTS.md files above the Git root as `harness: codex`, omits the global override, and shows a shadowed AGENTS.md beside its override. Presence-only scope limits the harm.
+  fix-shape hint: add `$CODEX_HOME/AGENTS.override.md`; bound the Codex-tagged walk at the Git root; add a one-line caveat that "present" is not "loaded" for shadowed or truncated files.
+- [Nit] §2.3 "`harness` one of `claude | codex` (the harness that reads that file name)" — A single `harness` value per file name misattributes AGENTS.md, which Claude Code also reads when no CLAUDE.md exists.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory: "Claude Code can read `AGENTS.md` as your project instructions"; table row "An `AGENTS.md`, and no `CLAUDE.md` or `CLAUDE.local.md` in your working directory or above it → Your `AGENTS.md`"; default setting `claude-md-or-agents-md`, configurable under `cc-plugin-agents-md@builtin`, which graph cannot see. A Claude seat with only AGENTS.md gets it tagged `codex` and unmarked as its own harness's file.
+  fix-shape hint: make `harness` a list or add a `shared` value for AGENTS.md; note the conditional read.
+- [Should-fix] §2.2 JSON `"instructions":{"status","loaded_rev","loaded_at","source","truncated","stale_parts"}`; task hg-blw.4 acceptance — The roster JSON `instructions` shape has no loaded-parts field, yet hg-blw.4 requires roster JSON to carry the loaded part labels, so the schema:1 contract and the task tree disagree.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: Spec line 206 shape has no `parts`; §2.1 line 162 "The JSON is the contract". `bd show hg-blw.4`: "roster JSON instructions carries the loaded part labels from the record, so roster answers which files each clone was handed". coverage-ledger.md C17 (r2, applied) records the bead change; the spec section was never updated. No view prints a clone's loaded parts: `stack` prints the HEAD manifest and `stale_parts` is a delta. hg-blw.1 reserves fields from the spec shape, so the label field would arrive unreserved.
+  fix-shape hint: add `parts:[{label}]` (or label list) to the §2.2 roster `instructions` JSON and say whether the `stack` clones section carries it.
+- [Nit] §2.1 "Each JSON document carries `schema: 1` and the `head` revision it was computed from"; Inventory "read once from HEAD"; HistoryWalker "walks `main` from HEAD" — Nothing requires one invocation to pin a single resolved HEAD for Inventory, per-seat renders, loaded comparison and history walks, so a concurrent writer commit can yield a torn view labelled with one `head`.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: Spec lines 443, 461, 466, 493 describe each component reading "at HEAD" separately; line 279 and 466 justify writer concurrency only by Git object immutability, which does not make separate HEAD resolutions agree. No abstraction takes a CommitId parameter. The ground seat notes the loaded-record comparison reads `.graph-local`, not Git, so that part of the claim is overstated.
+  fix-shape hint: one sentence: resolve HEAD to a CommitId once per invocation and pass it to Scope, Inventory, `seat_parts`, HistoryWalker and the Envelope.
+- [Should-fix] §2.4 "What it reads per commit: … the `ActionRecord`, when present"; JSON row `action:{id,kind,undoable,undone}`; sample "act_01… undone" — The log row's `action.undone` cannot come from the ActionRecord as it was in the commit that added it; `undone_by` is filled by a later undo commit, and the spec does not say which tree the walker reads it from.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: src/model/action.rs:30 `undone_by: Vec<OpId>`; src/undo/candidates.rs:184 `undone: !a.undone_by.is_empty()`; src/undo/compensate.rs:248-274 pushes the undoing op in a later commit. At the action's own commit `undone_by` is empty, so a per-commit read always yields `undone: false`, contradicting the §2.4 sample. Refute seat (CONFIRM) notes a cheap in-pass fix: the newest-first walk sees the undo (with `undoes=<act>`) before the original.
+  fix-shape hint: state the source: collect undone action ids from undo actions seen earlier in the same newest-first pass (noting the scan-cap/scope caveat), or read the action at HEAD and count that cost.
+- [Should-fix] §2.3 Status/unknown; hg-blw.11 second acceptance and hg-blw.4 status tests — The integration test for current→stale (R14) can pass while production is broken, if the test commits the occupant before the hook write, the opposite of the production order.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: setup_claude.rs `hook_command` runs `seat --hook-prompt; session-report --from-hook claude`; capture.rs:159-172 stamps `at = self.clock.now()` in the daemon; mutations.rs:694 sets `Occupant.since = at`. Neither the spec nor hg-blw.11/hg-blw.4 requires the test to drive `session-report` after the hook. A TestRoot that seeds the occupant first reaches `current`, which real startups never do. Companion of the Blocking finding above; the refute seat rejected only because the task text lives outside the spec file.
+  fix-shape hint: have the hg-blw.11 sweep run the real hook chain (`--hook-prompt`, then `session-report`) from a clone with no occupant and assert `current`; state the production order in §2.3.
+- [Should-fix] §2.4 Cost "scanned 5000 commits, older history not searched"; §2.4 Journal pointer to `ops`; §2.3 `@`-imports line; §2.5 tombstone footer — Notices the spec says the command prints have no defined stream and no JSON field, so `--json` consumers either get non-JSON on stdout or lose the information.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: §2.1 "The JSON is the contract; text is a rendering of it." The log row shape has no `scanned`/`stopped_early`; the sessions row and envelope have no tombstone count; §3 Envelope is "schema, head, live where applicable, body". The roster has `last_change: older than scan`, so a machine-readable form was contemplated but not extended to `log` or `sessions`. A `log --json` consumer cannot tell "no matching changes" from "scan cap hit".
+  fix-shape hint: add `scan:{scanned,stopped_early}` and `tombstones:N` to the envelope or body; route text-only notices to stderr or the text rendering.
+- [Should-fix] §2.4 Scope membership "A commit is in scope when any of these holds: … `--by <seat>`"; LogQuery "or (with `--by`) when the requester's seat matches" — The combination semantics of `--by <seat>` with a scope are contradictory: listed as an OR-alternative of scope membership while also described as a distinct "changes made by" view.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: Spec line 160 and 435 make omitted scope = Instance = everything, so `log --by b` under the OR reading matches every commit and `--by` filters nothing; `log seat a --by b` returns changes to a OR by b, contradicting §2.4's "changes made by … as opposed to the default changes to". Line 470 repeats the OR. No section states AND semantics or how a renamed/retired requester seat resolves.
+  fix-shape hint: one sentence: `--by` is an AND filter on requester seat id, resolved with the same resolver as scope.
+- [Should-fix] §2.2 With `--live`; Key abstractions Roster Errors "daemon down for `--live` is exit 5" — `roster --live` has no defined behaviour when the daemon is up but the Herdr snapshot fails or threads are unavailable.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: src/bootstrap/mod.rs:65-67 `herdr.snapshot().await.ok()?` swallows a snapshot failure into `None` and also returns None when `d.herdr` is absent; `roster.live` reuses the daemon's in-memory seat view and could copy that pattern, printing every clone with no agent. Spec lines 161, 189-193, 479-480 cover only daemon-down. Line 193 says `--live` is "the only way to answer 'is an agent running right now'", so a silent empty overlay is the dangerous default.
+  fix-shape hint: specify that a failed snapshot is an error (exit 5 or a distinct code) or sets an explicit `live_unavailable` flag in the envelope; never an empty overlay.
+- [Nit] §2.2 With --live "`live_pane_matches`: whether the snapshot still shows the committed binding"; §5 S1 unmanaged — `roster --live` unmanaged detection and `live_pane_matches` compare snapshot ids with committed bindings without accounting for the binding's Herdr incarnation; after a Herdr restart ids can name different objects.
+  verdict: confirmed (reproduce ✓ / refute ✗-survived / ground ✓)
+  evidence: src/model/common.rs ~86-130 `Binding.incarnation` and `Incarnation::same_server`: ids are meaningful only within the same server. Spec never mentions incarnation for matching, nor which record kinds (teamspace/seat `runtime` vs clone) count as binding a workspace or tab. Refute seat notes src/reconcile/planner.rs already filters with `same_server` at lines 294, 384, 435, 468, so the helper exists. JSON `{id,label}` does not say label may be null (src/ports/herdr.rs:36 `Option<String>`).
+  fix-shape hint: one sentence: matching and "bound" are incarnation-aware via `same_server` against the daemon's current incarnation; list the record kinds that bind each Herdr object; mark `label` nullable.
